Yes, that's right; lawful! Early in the game I found a HoOA, and
when the first high altar I reached turned out to be lawful, I
pulled it out along with my supply of holy water (It actually
took 5 or 6 tries). So call me a coward. I had come to the
conclusion that I was somewhat underprepared for the Astral
Plane, and I don't think I could have reached a second altar.

It wasn't my first ascension, but this is my first ascension
post. It was my sixth ascension. The first three were lawful
priests, the fourth was a lawful valkyrie, and the fifth was
a genocideless lawful priest. So although I spent most of this
game neutral, it's ironic that all my ascensions are still
lawful. All of them have been human, and all male except the
valkyrie.

Having learned the protection racket, I've been attempting
some of the "weaker" roles: A,H,T. I lost my good Arc to a
disk crash just before taking on Rodney, and my good but
underprepared Healer to YASD in Orcus-town. This was my
first Tourist to finish Sokoban, and he went all the way!

My other ascensions have been helped along by early good
fortune, an early wish or the gift of Grayswandir. The
luckiest break this game was finding a magic whistle in the
second room. This was pretty helpful since I'm a recent
convert to the protection racket, but the dive to Minetown
seemed longer and harder than usual regardless.

>From the first level of the mines I was quivering daggers and
wielding a dwarvish mattock (although I didn't actually use
them early on), and that didn't change for a long time. I
wrote the E-word with my fingers many many many times, and
let my pet take on everybody. I saved every dagger I found,
and gradually improved my armor with my pet's help.

After buying protection I levelled up until I felt reasonably
safe. The temple was co-aligned, but all I got was Cleaver.
I did Sokoban and finished the mines. Nothing really lucky
had happened yet, so I still wasn't too confident. I was
pleasantly surprised when I got dagger to Skilled for the
first time; I hadn't known about throwing more than one.
I got teleportitis in the mines from eating a tengu, and
this annoyed me a lot until I finally got TC from another
tengu on the quest.

I went through a lot of pets in the early game. I had gotten
used to playing Healers, who don't lose them so often.
Finally, whistling my dog Fido to a polymorph trap produced
Fido the baby green dragon. I took him to Sokoban to toughen
up, raising him to a level 22 green dragon with 176 HP. He
lasted a long time, until a black dragon disintegrated him
in the castle (Sob!). I avenged him by genociding black
dragons, but went petless thereafter.

I had the worst luck this game with identify scrolls. First
I didn't even start finding them until the quest. Then I'm
sure I read several dozen blessed scrolls of identify
throughout the game, and never once got the "identify
everything" effect. Usually I depend on that to tell me
how many charges my wands have. Maybe that's way I didn't
use the PYEC more.

On the quest locate level I hit a polymorph trap that turned
me into a xorn, destroying much of the good armor I had
collected. I didn't think to eat any rings or amulets, but
I used the phasing ability to explore the level quickly and
fight the soldiers on my own terms. Then the throne gave me
my first wish (GDSM), and the loss of my armor was no longer
a problem.

The Master of Thieves went down really easily; I hit him
with !oParalysis and chucked a few of my blessed +6 elven
daggers until he died. This is a huge contrast to my early
quest experiences versus Nalzok. Why do quest nemeses
vary so much in difficulty?

Sometime before the castle I gave up the dwarvish mattock
for Werebane (my second sacrifice gift) so I could wear a
shield of reflection. I was mostly throwing daggers,
anyway. Then sometime in Gehennom I decided more offensive
power was more important. I put away the shield and started
to #twoweapon Werebane and a silver saber. This seemed
particularly effective against Rodney, and I never missed
the reflection.

I spent as little time as possible in Moloch's Sanctum.
Using suggestions I had read in rgrn, after the invocation
I went down the stairs then back up, and read a cursed scroll
of teleportation. I appeared very close to the temple. I
dealt with the high priest the same way I had with the
Master of Thieves, grabbed and named the amulet, and drank
a cursed !oGL.

I had dropped 37 K-rations by one of the downstairs, but
in hurrying up I completely missed them. I had very little
trouble with the mysterious force; is this because I was
neutral?

I paused only briefly on level 1. I knew I couldn't afford to
lose my rings, so I put on an amulet of reflection I had found
in Gehennom. I collected and alchemized about a dozen blessed
!oFH. I tried wishing for Magicbane, but didn't get it. I
surely should have spent more time preparing, but I was
impatient and hurried to the planes.

I've decided the Plane of Air isn't nearly as scary as I
thought. I learned to wait around and heal inside
vortices, and my potions saved me a couple of times.

So before I knew it, there I was on Astral. Putting on my
blindfold, I headed for Pestilence. He's always been the
easiest for me; my blessed unicorn horn has never failed.
As the locals started whacking me and cursing my stuff,
I came to the decision that I was underprepared. I probably
could have used my 2 wands of death more, but I was worried
about #invoking the PYEC too often. It was a slow and
stressful journey, and I had to use more potions. I
zapped Pestilence with a wand of death, not wanting to take
chances.

At the next choke point I was surrounded by a lot of bad
guys. Somebody threw a potion of confusion at me, so I
thought I'd try something different. I pulled out a scroll
of taming and read it, confused. I thought I might get a pet
or two, but no such luck. It did make some of my attackers peaceful,
though. I cleared a path by killing the weakest
thing around, a naga. Then I was able to run to the temple.
I closed and locked the door behind me, then went to the
altar. My spirits sank when I saw it was lawful, then
suddenly I remembered the helmet!

So I took the easy way out; so what? I ascended a tourist!

So I started with the protection racket, playing like a
pacifist. Then later I was just a combat wombat. But this
is my first game of any real length in which I *never once*
successfully cast a spell.

